      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the largest prime divisor of \\(11! + 12!\\), we first need to factor out the common term in both the expression and the factorial. This can be done by observing:\n\n\\[11! = 1 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times \\ldots \\times 10 \\times 11\\]\n\\[12! = 1 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times \\ldots \\times 10 \\times 11 \\times 12\\]\n\nFactoring out the common term \\(11!\\) from the expression, we get:\n\n\\[11! + 12! = 11! \\times (1 + 12) = 11! \\times 13\\]\n\nNow, the next step is to prime factorize \\(13\\) since it is already a prime number. Hence, the largest prime divisor would be the prime factor of the resulting factor, which is \\(13\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\(\\boxed{13}\\)",
